				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/malycegraves" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4916887); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4916887&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606337834" alt="MalyceGraves" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4916887)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/malycegraves" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4916887); return false;" >MalyceGraves</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606322315 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Nov 2020 16:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4827943">
									

<p>I get the idea of having sexual predators becoming prey animals to an even more destructive force, but this doesn't sound like it's going to be</p>
<p>A) sensitive to the survivors of sexual assault, or</p>
<p>B) anything more than a mary-sue/X-man trope with a bit of sexual exploitation sprinkled on for spice</p>
<p>Topics like this are hard to write, even for experienced and established authors. Most people just stay away from these topics in the modern era of SCP authorship, and for good reason. I get the allure of wanting to explore darker aspects of human nature, and at least this idea isn't putting the reader in the shoes of someone that wants to commit these atrocious acts.</p>
<p>That being said, humanoids in general are also difficult to write because they have the conjoined issue of being both something we need to relate to in order to engage with the story, while also embodying characteristics that are inherently unrelatable.</p>
<p>Frankly, none of the way this has been presented gives me any confidence that this will be anything more than what I've already said.</p>

							</div>

								
					
